In Java, when you're working with streaming responses, you might encounter the need to handle End Of Stream (EOS) markers. EOS markers indicate the end of a stream, and it's essential to handle them properly to avoid errors or unexpected behavior in your application.
Here's an example of how you can handle EOS in streaming responses using Java:

### Using InputStream

When working with `InputStream`, you can use the `available()` method to check if there's more data available in the stream. If the method returns 0, it indicates that the end of the stream has been reached.

```java
import java.io.InputStream;

public class StreamingResponseHandler {
    public void handleStreamingResponse(InputStream inputStream) throws Exception {
        byte[] buffer = new byte[1024];
        int bytesRead;

        while ((bytesRead = inputStream.read(buffer)) != -1) {
            // Process the data in the buffer
            processBuffer(buffer, bytesRead);

            // Check if the end of the stream has been reached
            if (inputStream.available() == 0) {
                break; // EOS reached, exit the loop
            }
        }
    }

    private void processBuffer(byte[] buffer, int bytesRead) {
        // Implement your logic to process the data in the buffer
    }
}
```

### Using BufferedReader

When working with `BufferedReader`, you can use the `readLine()` method to read lines from the stream. If the method returns `null`, it indicates that the end of the stream has been reached.

```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;

public class StreamingResponseHandler {
    public void handleStreamingResponse(InputStream inputStream) throws Exception {
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(inputStream));
        String line;

        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            // Process the line
            processLine(line);
        }
    }

    private void processLine(String line) {
        // Implement your logic to process the line
    }
}
```

### Using Java 8's Stream API

Java 8's Stream API provides a more concise way to handle streaming responses. You can use the `readAllBytes()` method to read the entire stream into a byte array, or use the `lines()` method to read the stream line by line.

```java
import java.io.InputStream;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public class StreamingResponseHandler {
    public void handleStreamingResponse(InputStream inputStream) throws Exception {
        // Read the entire stream into a byte array
        byte[] bytes = inputStream.readAllBytes();
        // Process the byte array
        processByteArray(bytes);

        // Alternatively, read the stream line by line
        try (Stream<String> lines = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(inputStream)).lines()) {
            lines.forEach(this::processLine);
        }
    }

    private void processByteArray(byte[] bytes) {
        // Implement your logic to process the byte array
    }

    private void processLine(String line) {
        // Implement your logic to process the line
    }
}
```

In all cases, it's essential to handle the EOS marker properly to avoid errors or unexpected behavior in your application. By using the methods described above, you can ensure that your application handles streaming responses correctly and efficiently.
