kv_cache is a mechanism to improve the performance of decoder models by caching the key and value vectors of the self-attention mechanism. 

When the model is generating text, it needs to compute the attention weights for each token in the input sequence. The attention weights are computed based on the similarity between the query vector (the current token) and the key vectors (the previous tokens). 

The key vectors are typically computed only once for each token in the input sequence, and then reused for all subsequent tokens. This is because the key vectors do not change based on the query vector. 

However, in some cases, the model may need to generate text that is longer than the input sequence. In such cases, the model needs to compute the key vectors for the new tokens that are not present in the input sequence. 

kv_cache is a mechanism to cache the key and value vectors for the new tokens, so that they can be reused for subsequent tokens. This can improve the performance of the model by reducing the number of computations required. 

In the context of decoder models, kv_cache is typically used to cache the key and value vectors for the previous tokens, so that they can be reused for the next token. This can improve the performance of the model by reducing the number of computations required. 

